#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055571} :activity/id #uuid "95aa7191-773d-555a-b024-bb0d165f5cd9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050540 :topic/id #uuid "521360f4-4398-5d66-ae61-73dcd0f74f49" :topic/math-academy-id 51} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056045 :tutorial/content "Suppose $S$ is a set. The **power set** of $S,$ denoted $\\mathcal{P}(S),$ is the set containing every subset of $S,$ *including* the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the power set of the set\n\n$$S = \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$$\n\nTo do this, we list out all the subsets of $S{:}$\n\n- The subset containing no elements is $\\emptyset.$\n- The subsets containing $1$ element are $\\{ 1 \\},$ $\\{ 2 \\},$ and $\\{ 3 \\}.$\n- The subsets containing $2$ elements are $\\{ 1,2 \\},$ $\\{ 1,3 \\},$ and $\\{ 2,3 \\}.$\n- The subset containing $3$ elements is $S$ itself: $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$\n\nNow, let's put all these subsets together as elements of a single set. We get\n\n$$\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{ \\emptyset, \\{1\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{3\\}, \\{ 1,2 \\}, \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\{ 2,3 \\}, \\{ 1,2,3 \\} \\big\\}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cbbf2155-81d5-52d9-b9c0-2416958838b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8241 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cccf59fe-5d64-567d-835c-def5b2fb5c93" :step/math-academy-id 153365 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056060 :tutorial/content "Another way to generate all the subsets of $S=\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is using a tree diagram. We start off with the empty set $\\{\\}$ and generate additional subsets of $S$ by systematically inserting or excluding elements of $S.$\n\nLet's go through the process.\n\n- Start with the empty set $\\{\\}$ at the root node of our tree. This is the parent node.\n- Draw two branches pointing away from the parent node. The tip of each arrow gives a child node. Label one branch 'No' and the other 'Yes'.\n\n![](images/a4/a4b17d8114cc48cdc858d036b839d766dcb60f3b400a096406479c9daf7afac0.png)\n- We'll now write down a subset of $S$ at each child node:\n\n- At the child node (tip of the arrow) whose branch is labeled 'No,' we write down the parent node's set. $$\\{\\}$$\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *first* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$ $$\\{1\\}$$ We now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/61/61c6c192df26a22cac5dddad6b2692a519da055730fe9738f3173cb38229cf8b.png)\n\nWe repeat the process, setting the subsets found in the previous step as the new parent nodes.\n\n- We start by considering the parent node $\\{\\}{:}$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'No', we write down the parent node's set. $$\\{\\}$$\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *second* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$ $$\\{2\\}$$ We now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/6e/6e43b6022b5939b824995936d8ab910b7b3d2e821a6c96be63606245f6e60dbb.png)\n- Then, we consider the parent node $\\{1\\}{:}$\n\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'No', we write down the parent node's set. $$\\{1\\}$$\n- At the child node whose branch is labeled 'Yes,' we create a new set by inserting the *second* element of $S$ into the parent node's set. This gives the following subset of $S{:}$ $$\\{1,2\\}$$ We now have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/6f/6f81c80039f14260bdfdb8d55a34820874fe518e076d2718629d4faf385e92d5.png)\n- Finally, we repeat the process for the new parent nodes $\\{\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{1\\},$ and $\\{1,2\\}.$\n\nOur final tree diagram looks as follows.\n\n![](images/12/127ee1187ebf3a2cc498c88326285d5adf5d609cf3276885e8c9d525435133eb.png)\n\nThe final column lists all subsets of $S.$\n\n$$\\emptyset = \\{\\},\\quad \\{1\\},\\quad \\{2\\},\\quad \\{3\\},\\quad \\{ 1,2 \\},\\quad \\{ 1,3 \\},\\quad \\{ 2,3 \\},\\quad \\{ 1,2,3 \\}$$\n\nFinally then, we can write down $\\mathcal P(S){:}$\n\n$$\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{ \\emptyset, \\{1\\}, \\{2\\}, \\{3\\}, \\{ 1,2 \\}, \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\{ 2,3 \\}, \\{ 1,2,3 \\} \\big\\}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a2897eb-b185-5619-bc49-f2ef12df345a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8511 :tutorial/title "Using a Tree Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "72c3927c-894d-55ae-8094-79014856a6ad" :step/math-academy-id 153366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054637 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054446 :question/id #uuid "a138e1a7-52c5-5763-975e-cab082266099" :question/math-academy-id "e-13199"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f1f99d3-ebcb-50c6-a162-bd58f7d5ec92" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395714}] :knowledge-point/title "Listing the Elements in a Power Set"} :step/id #uuid "ba17ca05-c9c8-5cb0-9680-49c6db006781" :step/math-academy-id 153367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056046 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a finite set $S$ with cardinality $|S|.$ Then, the cardinality of $\\mathcal P(S)$ is given by\n\n$$\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|}.$$\n\nIn other words, a finite set with cardinality $|S|$ has $2^{|S|}$ subsets.\n\nFor example, for the set $S = \\{ a,b,c \\},$ we have $|S| = 3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|} = 2^3 = 8.$$\n\nThis tells us that $S$ has $8$ subsets, and consequently, $\\mathcal{P}(S)$ has $8$ elements.\n\n$$\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{\\emptyset}_1, \\underbrace{ \\{a\\}}_2, \\underbrace{ \\{b\\}}_3, \\underbrace{ \\{c\\}}_4, \\underbrace{ \\{ a,b \\}}_5, \\underbrace{ \\{ a,c \\}}_6, \\underbrace{ \\{ b,c \\}}_7, \\underbrace{ \\{ a,b,c \\}}_8 \\big\\}$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- It's clear from our rule that if $S$ is finite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is finite.\n- It can be shown that if $S$ is infinite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is also infinite.\n\nWe'll discuss power sets of infinite sets at the end of this lesson and in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "fa764524-a501-5804-b1a1-6523e739b234" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8242 :tutorial/title "The Cardinality of a Power Set"} :step/id #uuid "d9225952-f921-5ed9-a6b9-726af08be8b6" :step/math-academy-id 153371 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054638 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054447 :question/id #uuid "a72a78b7-eaef-59a3-a23a-82d291e8253a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13200"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e22a7b0-753b-51b1-8946-5d17456aa778" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382729}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cardinality of a Power Set"} :step/id #uuid "5f5b95d2-66ba-5245-bd79-47140cb7833a" :step/math-academy-id 153372 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054448 :question/id #uuid "b8db07f6-cdbf-5d00-8f3c-6f8b07277ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13201"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ec1dc0-4e21-5001-959e-ed35039ca00e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383680}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Cardinality in More Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "d77da74f-73f3-59f5-9290-13740989504d" :step/math-academy-id 153376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054640 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054449 :question/id #uuid "ceb40e91-12d8-5636-8b98-fa41aaca33b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13214"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383694}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"} :step/id #uuid "3544c1c7-cd8e-5b1d-a95a-e07e977f3121" :step/math-academy-id 153379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056061 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, our primary focus was on power sets of *finite* sets. The definition of the power set of an *infinite* set mirrors that of finite sets. Namely, it comprises all possible subsets.\n\nAs we already understand, for finite sets, the size of power sets grows exponentially based on the size of the underlying set:\n\n$$\\big| \\mathcal{P}(X) \\big| = 2^{|X|}$$\n\nWhen dealing with power sets of infinite sets, the situation is more intricate.\n\nFor example, let's consider $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ the set containing all points on the Cartesian plane. What are the elements of the power set $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb R^2)?$\n\nAny collection of points on the plane is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ Such subsets can be visualized as black-and-white images on the plane, where points belonging to the subset are drawn in black, and points not belonging to the subset are represented in white.\n\nFor instance, each of the following images gives us a specific element of $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^2){:}$\n\n![](images/bb/bbdb49640183563e0cb137b3c57095edd8b81bceac4dcda1bf3460371df17930.png)\n\nIt follows that any text, be it any or all existing Math Academy lessons, those planned for the future, or any other possible text (finite or infinite), including those not yet written, are all contained within $\\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^2).$\n\n![](images/c4/c47487cf1a7253bfafd5419cd824df30669a575d4b90f3fddc36566e920325bc.png)\n\nIt's somewhat extraordinary that such a vast and important set can be expressed using just the symbols $\\mathcal P(\\mathbb R^2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca3f303c-7c55-5abe-945a-dfe8a4816aa8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8512 :tutorial/title "Power Sets of Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "3f3fd95b-66c4-5c77-81de-9a4ba5c39f8e" :step/math-academy-id 153382}] :activity/title "Power Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054910}]}
